WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:02.240 --> 00:00:04.640
You've said a couple of times now that when the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:04.640 --> 00:00:08.960
opportunity presents itself you'll speak with&nbsp;
Emmanuel Macron, but have you reached out?

00:00:08.960 --> 00:00:10.000
Have you offered to,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:10.560 --> 00:00:15.200
to have you tried to call him and speak to&nbsp;
him now, and is he just not taking your call?

00:00:15.200 --> 00:00:19.680
Yes we have and the opportunity for that&nbsp;
call is not yet, but we'll be patient.

00:00:19.680 --> 00:00:24.720
We understand the disappointment and that&nbsp;
is the way you manage difficult issues.

00:00:24.720 --> 00:00:26.240
It's a difficult decision.

00:00:26.240 --> 00:00:30.720
It's a very difficult decision, and of&nbsp;
course we had to weigh up what would be&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:30.720 --> 00:00:34.960
the the obvious disappointment to France,&nbsp;
but at the end of the day as a government&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:34.960 --> 00:00:40.000
we have to do what is right for Australia and&nbsp;
serve Australia's national security interests,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:40.000 --> 00:00:45.120
and I will always choose Australia's&nbsp;
national security interests first.

00:00:45.120 --> 00:00:49.040
A couple of weeks ago, Australia, the&nbsp;
United States, and the United Kingdom&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:49.040 --> 00:00:51.360
announced a new security pact called AUKUS,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:51.360 --> 00:00:55.040
which will result in the three countries&nbsp;
collaborating in the Indo-pacific region.

00:00:55.840 --> 00:01:00.400
The announcement of AUKUS sets up a financial&nbsp;
and strategic loss for France, and it also&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:00.400 --> 00:01:05.520
represents an increasing investment by a subset&nbsp;
of western democracies in a specific region,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:05.520 --> 00:01:07.680
and it looks like Europe isn't being included

00:01:11.360 --> 00:01:15.680
China's military is growing and it currently&nbsp;
has the largest naval force on the planet.

00:01:15.680 --> 00:01:20.160
In 2016, Australia, equipped with submarines&nbsp;
that were nearing their end of service&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:20.160 --> 00:01:22.240
and also feeling the growing pressure from China,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:22.240 --> 00:01:27.520
secured a 90 billion submarine deal with the&nbsp;
French naval defense contractor Naval Group.

00:01:27.520 --> 00:01:31.440
And this deal would give Australia&nbsp;
possession of 12 shortfin barracuda-based&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:31.440 --> 00:01:33.600
attack-class diesel-electric submarines.

00:01:34.240 --> 00:01:38.320
The diesel as opposed to nuclear-powered subs&nbsp;
would have been cheaper to run and maintain,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:38.320 --> 00:01:40.240
but they would have needed to resurface more,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:40.240 --> 00:01:42.560
be smaller, and would have had&nbsp;
a weaker propulsion system.

00:01:43.120 --> 00:01:47.360
The French deal became precarious after a lack&nbsp;
of demand for local Australian industry wasn't&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:47.360 --> 00:01:52.800
fulfilled, along with cost overruns, delays, and&nbsp;
an increasing demand for more powerful submarines.

00:01:53.440 --> 00:01:56.880
Nuclear-powered subs can remain&nbsp;
submerged for longer amounts of time,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:56.880 --> 00:02:00.960
are larger, and are also more expensive,&nbsp;
but when it comes to submerged propulsion&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:00.960 --> 00:02:04.480
(speed underwater) nuclear power&nbsp;
is simply the better option.

00:02:04.480 --> 00:02:08.480
The value of the nuclear subs seems to have&nbsp;
outweighed the value of the diesel ones,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:08.480 --> 00:02:10.080
and Australia has announced that their deal with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:10.080 --> 00:02:13.200
France is over as they put in a&nbsp;
new order with the United States&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:13.200 --> 00:02:22.320
for at least eight nuclear-powered hunter-killer&nbsp;
submarines as part of their new security pact.

00:02:22.320 --> 00:02:25.840
Across the world are putting their&nbsp;
attention towards the Indo-Pacific.

00:02:25.840 --> 00:02:30.640
A pretty large place it's home to sixty percent&nbsp;
of the world's population, produces sixty percent&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:30.640 --> 00:02:35.920
of the global GDP, and it's also the second&nbsp;
largest destination for exports from the EU.

00:02:35.920 --> 00:02:39.200
For member states like France, a country&nbsp;
with more than a million citizens in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:39.200 --> 00:02:43.520
the area as well as stationed troops,&nbsp;
their objectives have been made clear.

00:02:43.520 --> 00:02:45.520
France wants to be a stabilizing force,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:45.520 --> 00:02:49.840
promote the values of freedom and the rule of law,&nbsp;
and help solve certain geopolitical challenges.

00:02:51.040 --> 00:02:55.120
For the EU specifically, by pivoting off&nbsp;
of individual member states' own goals&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:55.120 --> 00:02:58.720
as well as being primarily led by France,&nbsp;
the EU has announced that they will focus&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:58.720 --> 00:03:02.560
on reinforcing cooperation, promote&nbsp;
a rules-based international order,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:02.560 --> 00:03:05.760
keep open markets, and ensure&nbsp;
stable trading environment.

00:03:05.760 --> 00:03:09.680
However, unfortunately for them on the same&nbsp;
day that the EU was set to make their official&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:09.680 --> 00:03:13.840
announcement for their Indo-pacific&nbsp;
strategy, AUKUS was also announced.

00:03:13.840 --> 00:03:16.080
And that means that the three countries that were&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:16.080 --> 00:03:19.920
already notorious for being excessively&nbsp;
loud when it's not socially acceptable&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:19.920 --> 00:03:24.000
decided to announce their own private clubhouse&nbsp;
in the indo-pacific region – overshadowing the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:24.000 --> 00:03:27.520
EU's announcement, and forcing europe&nbsp;
to reconsider their overall strategy

00:03:31.280 --> 00:03:35.920
Macron has already begun signaling for a larger&nbsp;
push in European sovereignty –&nbsp;saying things&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:35.920 --> 00:03:40.880
like europeans must stop being naive, when we are&nbsp;
under pressure from powers which at times harden,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:40.880 --> 00:03:44.800
we need to react and show that we have the&nbsp;
power and capacity to defend ourselves.

00:03:44.800 --> 00:03:49.200
This statement happened alongside a new defense&nbsp;pact announced with Greece where France will&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:49.200 --> 00:03:54.000
supply the eastern european country with french&nbsp;
frigates supplied by, of course, Naval Group.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:54.800 --> 00:04:00.160
Recently, Charles Michel, the European Council&nbsp;President also declared that 2022 will be the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:00.160 --> 00:04:04.800
year of European Defense: we want less dependency;&nbsp;we want more influence because we have values&nbsp;to promote

00:04:04.800 --> 00:04:10.240
Next year, France will also take&nbsp;
over the rotating presidency of the council.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:10.240 --> 00:04:13.040
Although this mainly grants France&nbsp;
some form of agenda setting, it's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:13.040 --> 00:04:15.840
already clear that the french will&nbsp;
set the focus of their presidency&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:15.840 --> 00:04:21.040
on power and defense with the european defense&nbsp;
summit also being led by Macron in 2022

00:04:24.400 --> 00:04:31.280
If there is any time to look at how a european&nbsp;
army might develop, that time is now. 

00:04:31.280 --> 00:04:36.160
Back in 2020,&nbsp;EU defense ministers began working on a strategic&nbsp;
compass for security and defense. 

00:04:36.160 --> 00:04:41.520
The adoption&nbsp;will happen in the first half of 2022, alongside&nbsp;
France's presidency, and in that document we should&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:41.520 --> 00:04:46.400
be able to see europe's vision, not necessarily of&nbsp;
the entire population, but definitely of europe's&nbsp;political establishment

00:04:46.400 --> 00:04:52.000
For what kind of security&nbsp;
and defense actor Europe will be. 

00:04:52.000 --> 00:04:57.040
Common defense&nbsp;policies fall under member states, which means that&nbsp;
they're essentially gridlocked – a single veto can&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:57.040 --> 00:05:02.240
block its creation. Although the adoption of&nbsp;
the european army is highly improbable now,&nbsp;

00:05:02.240 --> 00:05:06.000
there are steps that set a groundwork&nbsp;
where it becomes increasingly more likely.

00:05:06.880 --> 00:05:12.400
For example, like in many other policy domains,&nbsp;Europe has fragmented military industries that can&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:12.400 --> 00:05:18.720
cause coordination problems. Take for example Card,

00:05:18.720 --> 00:05:26.800
 

00:05:26.800 --> 00:05:33.280
 

00:05:33.280 --> 00:05:40.640
 

00:05:40.640 --> 00:05:46.800
&nbsp;

00:05:46.800 --> 00:05:53.200
 

00:05:53.200 --> 00:05:57.760
CARD has recommened a joint development of different military defense&nbsp;technologies because a lack of coherence can&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:57.760 --> 00:06:02.960
have a negative impact on the ability of member&nbsp;states forces to operate together efficiently.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:02.960 --> 00:06:07.600
An example of this is the European main battle tank, which is a combination of military technology&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:07.600 --> 00:06:12.240
between Krauss Maffei of germany and&nbsp;
Nexter Systems of France.

00:06:12.240 --> 00:06:16.720
Although it's too early to say anything substantial about how this&nbsp;
will contribute to European defense concretely,&nbsp;

00:06:16.720 --> 00:06:20.880
it's a clear example of how fragmentation&nbsp;of the military in europe can be changed.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:21.840 --> 00:06:26.640
Overall a construction of a european army is likely to mirror other examples of where Europe&nbsp;has integrated:

00:06:26.640 --> 00:06:31.760
Small incremental steps that&nbsp;
make a larger coordinated European policy that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:31.760 --> 00:06:47.840
ends up replacing national fragmentation&nbsp;
seem like it was always to be expected.

00:07:00.000 --> 00:07:00.500
 

